---
import ToolLayout from '@/layouts/ToolLayout.astro'
import CalcMoneyMarginMarkup from '@/components/tools/finance/CalcMoneyMarginMarkup.tsx'
---

<ToolLayout slug="margin-and-markup-calculator">
  <CalcMoneyMarginMarkup client:load variant="both" />

  <Fragment slot="explainer">
    <h2>The same profit, two different denominators</h2>
    <p>
      Margin and markup measure the same money against different bases. Both start from the same
      gross profit (selling price minus cost), and divide it by a different number:
    </p>
    <pre><code>margin % = profit ÷ selling price × 100
markup % = profit ÷ cost × 100</code></pre>
    <p>
      Buy for 40, sell for 100. The profit is 60 either way. As a share of the 100 you took, that is a
      <strong>60% margin</strong>. As a share of the 40 you spent, it is a <strong>150% markup</strong>.
      Both numbers are correct for the same transaction. So a supplier and an accountant can describe
      the same deal and seem to disagree by a factor of two and a half.
    </p>

    <h2>The mistake that costs money</h2>
    <p>
      "We work on 30%" is ambiguous, and that is expensive. 30% added to a cost of 70 gives a price of
      91 and a margin of 23.1%, not 30%. To earn a 30% margin on that cost, the price must be 100, a
      markup of 42.9%.
    </p>
    <p>
      For a business with a turnover of a million, a 30% markup mistaken for a 30% margin is about
      70,000 of gross profit that does not exist. It is the most common pricing error in small
      business, and it continues because both numbers sound reasonable.
    </p>

    <h2>Conversion table</h2>
    <div class="overflow-x-auto">
      <table class="w-full min-w-[420px] border-collapse text-sm">
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th>Markup on cost</th>
            <th style="text-align:right">Equals margin</th>
            <th style="text-align:right">Price multiplier</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody class="tabular-nums">
          <tr><td>10%</td><td style="text-align:right">9.1%</td><td style="text-align:right">× 1.10</td></tr>
          <tr><td>20%</td><td style="text-align:right">16.7%</td><td style="text-align:right">× 1.20</td></tr>
          <tr><td>25%</td><td style="text-align:right">20.0%</td><td style="text-align:right">× 1.25</td></tr>
          <tr><td>33.3%</td><td style="text-align:right">25.0%</td><td style="text-align:right">× 1.33</td></tr>
          <tr><td>50%</td><td style="text-align:right">33.3%</td><td style="text-align:right">× 1.50</td></tr>
          <tr><td>66.7%</td><td style="text-align:right">40.0%</td><td style="text-align:right">× 1.67</td></tr>
          <tr><td>100%</td><td style="text-align:right">50.0%</td><td style="text-align:right">× 2.00</td></tr>
          <tr><td>150%</td><td style="text-align:right">60.0%</td><td style="text-align:right">× 2.50</td></tr>
          <tr><td>233%</td><td style="text-align:right">70.0%</td><td style="text-align:right">× 3.33</td></tr>
          <tr><td>400%</td><td style="text-align:right">80.0%</td><td style="text-align:right">× 5.00</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p>
      The two conversions are <code>margin = markup ÷ (1 + markup)</code> and
      <code>markup = margin ÷ (1 − margin)</code>, both with the percentages expressed as decimals.
    </p>

    <h2>Why margin cannot exceed 100% and markup can</h2>
    <p>
      Margin's denominator is the price, and the cost must fit inside that price, so profit can come
      near the price but never reach it. Markup's denominator is the cost, which has no such limit: a
      5 item sold for 50 is a 900% markup and a 90% margin. So high-margin sectors quote margin and
      low-cost sectors quote markup. Each picks the number that sounds better.
    </p>

    <h2>Which one to use, and when</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>
        Use markup to set a price. You start from a known cost and need a number to multiply by.
        Cost-plus pricing is markup.
      </li>
      <li>
        Use margin to judge a business. Gross margin is comparable across products and competitors,
        and it goes into a P&amp;L. Investors, lenders and accountants all use margin.
      </li>
      <li>
        Never mix them in one conversation. Agree which one you mean before you agree the number.
      </li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Pricing a whole list at once</h2>
    <p>
      The bulk panel takes a pasted column of costs (with or without item names, separated by comma,
      tab or semicolon) and prices each row at the margin or markup you set. Each row shows its price,
      profit and both percentages, and the table exports to CSV, Excel or PDF. It reads the last
      numeric field on a line as the cost and the first non-numeric field as the name, so you can paste
      a spreadsheet column without edits.
    </p>

    <h2>Gross, not net</h2>
    <p>
      All figures here are gross margin on the cost of goods. Rent, salaries, marketing, shipping,
      payment-processing fees, returns, shrinkage and tax still come out of that gross profit. A
      business can have a healthy gross margin and a negative net margin, so gross margin is a pricing
      tool, not a judgement on the business.
    </p>
    <p>
      Price on the net figure, before VAT or sales tax. Tax you collect for an authority is not your
      revenue and must never be in a margin calculation.
    </p>

    <h2>Your cost prices are not sent anywhere</h2>
    <p>
      Supplier costs are among the most sensitive numbers a business has. In this margin and markup
      calculator, JavaScript in your browser computes the pricing, the bulk table and the exports. To
      confirm, disconnect from the network: everything still works.
    </p>
  </Fragment>
</ToolLayout>
